Phase 1: Testing harness (src/ontology_spider/harness/ + harness/ corpus dir)

Stdlib only. The harness is a product feature (agents and CI both use it), not just test scaffolding.

Component	What it does
Scenario format (*.scenario.json, or .dialog with # expect: annotations)	Model source, ordered turns, expected answers, expected rules and fact IDs, expected violations. Golden files are produced from reviewed runs.
Runner harness run [--backend prolog|python|both] [--transport inproc|mcp]	Runs scenarios and writes a JSON + Markdown report (pass/fail, diffs, timings).
Differential / parity mode harness parity	Runs every query on both backends and diffs answers as sets, plus paths. This is the check that would have caught D3.
Generators harness gen chain|tree|lattice|random_dag|cyclic --size N --seed S	Deterministic synthetic ontologies for scale and edge cases, with seeded random.
Property checks (stdlib, seeded)	Round-trip json → model → json is identity. dialog export re-parses to an equal model. Ancestors are a superset of direct parents. Adding a cycle edge always triggers cycle_violation.
Bench harness bench --budget bench_budgets.json	p50/p95 latency per operation × size. Output is a JSON baseline committed to harness/baselines/, and CI fails on a regression beyond the budget.
MCP contract suite (enabled in Phase 3)	Same scenarios over stdio, plus protocol checks: initialize, tools/list schema validity, error codes, cancellation.
Phase 2: Engine core
reasoning/base.py: a Reasoner protocol with query(model, QuerySpec) -> ReasonerResult, plus capabilities.
Move the degraded BFS into reasoning/python_backend.py as a first-class PythonReasoner. Selection order: explicit choice, then swipl if present, then Python.
Write docs/semantics.md: the canonical meaning of each query kind, including dedup, full transitive subclass closure, and ordering. Then fix rules.pl to match. (This needs your call; see Decisions.)
engine.py: Engine, SessionManager, and Session (session_id, model, history, frames, last_result, an optional elicitation sub-state). Results use a single JSON-serializable QueryResult schema, so CLI, API, and MCP all emit the same shape.
Split ElicitationManager.step() into per-state handlers. Do this now, while the harness is coming online to catch regressions.
Phase 3: MCP server (src/ontology_spider/mcp/)
Transport: stdio, newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0. Implements initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call, resources/list, resources/read, and notifications/cancelled. Hand-rolling this in stdlib takes roughly 300–400 LOC. Streamable HTTP can come later behind the same dispatcher.
Entry points: dialog mcp [--model PATH] [--backend …] [--read-only] [--root DIR] and python -m ontology_spider.mcp.
Tools (each returns the Phase 2 QueryResult / JSON schema):
Tool	Purpose
session_open / session_close / session_list	Lifecycle. Optionally preload a model path or inline text.
model_load	Load a .dialog or .json file, or inline text, into a session (paths confined to --root).
assert_triple / declare_entity / retract	Writes, provenance-tagged with source=mcp:<client>. Disabled under --read-only.
run_dialog	Run a multi-line .dialog script and return per-turn results.
query	Any query form. Returns answers, explanations, and provenance.
explain	Proof trace for the last query in the session.
validate	Returns structured violations.
export	json / dialog / dot / html.
focus / constrain / where_am_i	Traversal frame stack.
engine_info	Version, active backend, swipl availability, capabilities.
Resources: ontology://session/{id}/model.json, ontology://examples/{name}, ontology://docs/language-reference.
Trust boundary: file access confined to --root, writes off under --read-only, per-call timeout and inference cap, and a max sessions and max assertions limit per session. Record all of this in architecture.md.
Brain Harness: Add an MCP connection config next to the existing plugin so the harness can use the engine over MCP as well as through the in-process adapter.
Phase 4: Performance
Lever	Expected gain
Persistent PrologWorker: a long-lived swipl process that loads rules once, reloads facts only when the model version changes (incrementally via assertz/retract where possible), and takes goals over stdin/stdout JSON lines. Small pool with a per-instance lock instead of the class lock.	~210 ms → low single-digit ms per query
Tabling (:- table transitive_reach/3) for reachability. Reconstruct proof paths from BFS parent pointers rather than enumerating all paths.	Exponential → polynomial on lattices
Validator: Tarjan SCC for cycles (report one witness cycle per SCC), memoized superclass closure, disjointness computed per class and not per (constraint × entity).	0.56 s at 64 assertions → milliseconds at 10k
Model: zero-copy read views (MappingProxyType), an iter_assertions() API, and a predicate-subject adjacency index	Removes O(N) copies in hot loops
Harness-driven budgets (proposed): Prolog query p50 < 15 ms; Python query on 10k assertions p50 < 5 ms; validate 10k-assertion lattice < 1 s; full test suite < 4 s	Enforced in CI by harness bench
Phase 5: Direction (candidate backlog, ordered by the harness data you'll have by then)
Multi-turn retraction and amendment over MCP (retract_by_turn is already in the model).
Query language growth: multi-constraint recommend, path from X to Y, negation (not instance_of), and counting.
Persistent sessions (save and restore to .dialog or JSON), so an agent can resume work.
Incremental validation (validate only the delta since the last version).
Re-test the Track 10 deferrals (OWL2, Datalog) against measured harness data, using their existing un-defer conditions.
5. Decisions needed from you

IMPORTANT

MCP implementation vs. the zero-dependency guardrail. The official mcp Python SDK is a third-party dependency (it pulls in pydantic, anyio, and more). Options:

(Recommended) Hand-roll the stdio MCP server in stdlib. The guardrail stays intact, and the protocol surface needed is small.
Put the official SDK in an optional extra, pip install ontology-spider[mcp], with the core staying dependency-free.
Both: stdlib stdio now, optional SDK-backed HTTP transport later.

